<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!-- Matches whatever corner radius the launcher uses for widget backgrounds. -->
    <dimen name="widget_corner_radius">@android:dimen/system_app_widget_background_radius</dimen>

    <!--
      How much room the rows get: the two paddings WidgetRenderer chooses between, and
      the floor a label is drawn from.

      The compact padding is what makes three rows fit a one-row cell: at 6dp a 68dp
      cell has 56dp inside it, which is the whole reason the "until" line is reachable
      at 2x1.

      A label has a floor and no ceiling, and neither is a size per cell size: 12sp is the
      smallest a label may be drawn at all, because a 2x1 has no surplus to grow into and
      is still the size a caption has to be legible at, and how far above it one goes is
      a proportion of the value beside it, measured by WidgetRenderer against the cell in
      front of it. A ceiling in dp here would be the trap described below in miniature —
      a size past which a widget dragged out larger stops changing.

      They are dimens rather than literals because both sides need the same numbers:
      styles.xml and widget_preview.xml to declare what a raw inflation shows,
      WidgetRenderer to measure against. A number written twice is a number that ends
      up meaning two things.
    -->
    <dimen name="widget_padding">10dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="widget_padding_compact">6dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="widget_label_text_min">12sp</dimen>

    <!--
      The value's box, and the range searched inside it.

      The two text sizes are read from both sides for the same reason as the metrics
      above: WidgetRenderer searches between them for the static value, and styles.xml
      hands them to `autoSizeM(in|ax)TextSize` for the ticking one, which sizes itself.

      The maximum is a sanity bound, never the size a cell settles on. What limits the
      text is the cell — how wide it is, and how much height is left once the labels have
      taken their share — and a maximum low enough to bind is a size past which a widget
      dragged out larger stops changing, growing only its margins. So it goes above what
      any cell can ask for: 160sp is where a countdown stops being a number on a home
      screen and starts being a poster, and the width of a phone's widest widget runs out
      well before it.

      The height is only the default the layouts declare — every variant the renderer
      builds sets its own, measured against the cell it is for. 56dp is a sane default
      for a raw inflation, not a bound on anything.
    -->
    <dimen name="widget_value_height">56dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="widget_value_text_min">11sp</dimen>
    <dimen name="widget_value_text_max">160sp</dimen>

    <dimen name="screen_margin">16dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="section_spacing">20dp</dimen>
</resources>
